WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.194
Hosting model and render mode shape the
security boundary for a Blazor application.

2
00:00:08.274 --> 00:00:15.643
The same component model can be used in
designs where interaction is processed on

3
00:00:15.723 --> 00:00:21.575
the server, where code runs in the browser
through WebAssembly,

4
00:00:21.655 --> 00:00:26.857
or where static and interactive rendering
are combined.

5
00:00:26.937 --> 00:00:32.247
Those choices affect where code runs,
where data appears,

6
00:00:32.327 --> 00:00:36.770
and which layer must enforce trusted
decisions.

7
00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:40.426
In server-side interactive behavior,

8
00:00:40.506 --> 00:00:45.383
UI events are processed through a server
connection.

9
00:00:45.463 --> 00:00:51.531
The browser displays the interface and
sends interaction events,

10
00:00:51.611 --> 00:00:56.163
but protected decisions can remain on the
server.

11
00:00:56.243 --> 00:01:02.962
This can be useful when the application
needs server-side access to data,

12
00:01:03.042 --> 00:01:06.510
policy, services, or configuration,

13
00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:12.116
but the design still needs authentication,
authorization,

14
00:01:12.196 --> 00:01:17.181
connection handling, logging, and safe
data shaping.

15
00:01:17.261 --> 00:01:24.847
In Blazor WebAssembly, application code is
delivered to and executed in the user's

16
00:01:24.927 --> 00:01:25.794
browser.

17
00:01:25.874 --> 00:01:31.184
That code should be treated like any other
browser client.

18
00:01:31.264 --> 00:01:35.274
It can improve the experience and call
APIs,

19
00:01:35.354 --> 00:01:41.423
but it should not contain secrets or
trusted authorization logic.

20
00:01:41.503 --> 00:01:47.896
Anything shipped to the browser should be
assumed visible to the user.

21
00:01:47.976 --> 00:01:52.203
Blazor Web Apps add more render-mode
choices,

22
00:01:52.283 --> 00:01:57.918
including static rendering, interactive
server rendering,

23
00:01:57.998 --> 00:02:04.392
interactive WebAssembly rendering, and
interactive auto behavior.

24
00:02:04.472 --> 00:02:09.240
These modes can be mixed across pages or
components.

25
00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:16.689
A design that mixes modes should document
where each sensitive workflow runs and

26
00:02:16.769 --> 00:02:20.562
where protected decisions are enforced.

27
00:02:20.642 --> 00:02:24.977
Render mode choices also affect data
exposure.

28
00:02:25.057 --> 00:02:31.559
A component should receive only the data
it needs to render and operate.

29
00:02:31.639 --> 00:02:38.032
Sensitive records, administrative fields,
server-only decisions,

30
00:02:38.112 --> 00:02:45.373
and internal diagnostic values should not
be sent to components that do not need

31
00:02:45.453 --> 00:02:45.995
them.

32
00:02:46.075 --> 00:02:51.927
Data minimization matters even when the
user is authenticated.

33
00:02:52.007 --> 00:03:00.135
Development convenience should not
determine production render mode or authorization

34
00:03:00.215 --> 00:03:00.973
design.

35
00:03:01.053 --> 00:03:08.097
A render mode that is easy during
prototyping may not be the right boundary for

36
00:03:08.177 --> 00:03:09.369
production.

37
00:03:09.449 --> 00:03:14.976
Teams should review the hosting model,
data flow, API shape,

38
00:03:15.056 --> 00:03:22.100
browser-delivered assets, and
authorization enforcement before release.
